Robotic grasping remains a persistent challenge in robotics due to factors such as diverse object geometries, textures, and the inherent limitations of current grasp prediction models. To address this, we present DexGraspNet, a high-fidelity, extensive synthetic grasp database designed to enhance robotic grasping performance. This dataset comprises 10 million diverse and complex grasp instances featuring two-degree-of-freedom objects. Its key attributes include: (1) a broad distribution of grasp poses derived from a large collection of grasp tools, (2) the inclusion of object deformations to boost generalization across deformable objects, and (3) the provision of diverse, high-fidelity grasp tool representations. Furthermore, DexGraspNet facilitates the simulation of real-world variations, such as object deformations and lighting changes, through two specific splits: (2) a Deformable Grasp Dataset and (3) an Illumination Grasp Dataset. Additionally, we introduce a novel Grasp Quality Index to quantitatively assess grasp stability, which was utilized during the generation of grasp instances. Finally, we demonstrate the utility of DexGraspNet for robotic skill learning by training and evaluating a grasp policy on this dataset using PPO and SAC algorithms. The dataset and associated code can be accessed at: https://github.com/ChenchenZhang/DexGraspNet. Please cite us if DexGraspNet assists your research.
